.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-SET-LOG-LEVEL 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-set-log-level \- turn an application's own logging up, down or off
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap set-log-level
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-level | \-l
.I LEVEL
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the level an application's own output is logged at.
.PP
Whatever an application writes to standard output or standard error is
read back by the manager and logged on a channel of its own,
.IR app.<application\-id> ,
verbatim. Standard error is recorded as an error and standard output as
information, so
.B \-\-level error
leaves what went wrong visible while silencing the rest, and
.B \-\-level off
silences the application entirely.
.PP
It takes effect on the manager's next reconcile \- within a few seconds
\- and restarts nothing. A log level is not a change of definition:
unlike
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
which restarts the application because the artifact, command,
environment and credentials a process was handed are all decided when it
starts, this leaves every instance running and every request in flight
alone. That is deliberate, since the reason to reach for it is usually
that something is going wrong
.I now
and the log either says too little about it or so much that nothing
else can be read.
.PP
The level is stored with the application, so it survives a restart of
the installation and of the manager.
.B \-\-level default
takes the setting back, leaving the application under whatever
.I euclid.logging.channels
says \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
under
.BR "APPLICATION OUTPUT" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application. Required.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-level " " \fILEVEL\fR
One of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR, \fIoff\fR, or \fIdefault\fR to take the
setting back. Required. Anything else is refused rather than
interpreted: a mistyped level quietly meaning \fIinfo\fR would be an
application logging more than was asked for, and quietly meaning
\fIoff\fR would be silence nobody asked for at all.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The level was recorded; the application, the level and the channel it
applies to are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No application of that name exists, the level was not one, the server
could not be reached, or the caller is not an administrator.
.SH EXAMPLES
Silence one application that is logging a line per request, then put it
back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 eap set-log-level \e
    --application-id parser --level off

euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 eap set-log-level \e
    --application-id parser --level default
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep only what an application writes to standard error, while it is
misbehaving:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 eap set-log-level \e
    --application-id pim-writer --level error
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-applications (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1)
